/// A token that stands in for a person who has no account.
///
/// ─── The most-copied shape in the tree, and nobody copied it ───────────────
///
/// Eleven instances were read before this was written, and the columns barely
/// move between them: calendly `BookingGrant`, jazzhr `ApplicationGrant`,
/// notion `PageLink`, portal `SharedSearch` and `Pass`, transit `ShareLink`,
/// connectteam `KioskDevice`, basecamp `Invitation`, example `CartGrant`, and
/// outside this repo cal.com `HashedLink` and documenso `DocumentShareLink`.
/// Each was written from memory and each spells the hashed column its own
/// way — `tokenHash`, `digest`, `token @guarded`, `token @secret` — which is
/// why no drift rule could ever have matched them.
///
/// This file is a TRAIT and not a model, because the shape never stands
/// alone: it is always spliced into the row the token stands for (the
/// booking, the application, the page link) or into a row of its own that
/// points at one. `@@trait(Grant)` is the whole install.
///
/// ─── The half that is not columns ─────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// A grant is only useful with the three lines that make a bearer of it a
/// principal, and those are the host schema's to write, because they name
/// the host's own key:
///
///     claim bookingId
///     @@allow('read',   id == auth().bookingId)
///     @@allow('update', id == auth().bookingId)
///
/// The claim is minted by the app when it issues the token, lands in the
/// session, and is what the row policy compares against. Nothing in this
/// trait can state it, because a trait cannot know what the host's id is
/// called. The one instance that forgot the policy (portal `Pass`) is a
/// table of tokens nothing reads by.
///
/// ─── What is deliberately NOT here ────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// **No `token`.** The plain token exists for one instant, in the response
/// that issues it, and is never stored. `@guarded` on the hash is what keeps
/// the hash out of every read and out of the audit trail (Invariant 7).
///
/// **No `capabilities`.** jazzhr and basecamp carry `Capability[]` on the
/// grant so one token may do less than the row's full policy allows. That is
/// a real need and a product decision; it is one column in the host when a
/// product has it, and a trait that ships it makes every other grant look
/// scoped when it is not.
///
/// **No `createdBy`.** Who issued it is `@@trail(audit)` on the host, which
/// records the create with its actor for free.
trait Grant {
  /// The hash of the token the bearer holds. `@unique` because a lookup by
  /// hash is the only read this column ever has; `@guarded` because the hash
  /// is as good as the token to anyone who can read the row.
  tokenHash  String    @unique @guarded

  /// Optional, and the instances disagree: basecamp requires it (an invitation
  /// that never expires is a standing hole), everyone else leaves it nullable
  /// (a booking's manage-link lives as long as the booking). The trait takes
  /// the permissive shape; a host that means `required` overrides the field,
  /// which is the one case where the host winning a trait field is the point.
  expiresAt  DateTime?

  /// Set by the owner, never cleared. A revoked grant is kept rather than
  /// deleted so the audit trail still names what the token could reach.
  revokedAt  DateTime?

  /// Stamped by the exchange, which is `@system` because a bearer must not be
  /// able to rewrite their own trail. transit was the only instance to record
  /// it and the only one that could answer "was this link ever opened".
  lastUsedAt DateTime? @system

  /// 8 across the board. Nothing but `asSystem()` writes a grant — the issuing
  /// service mints the hash — and a row a bearer could create, read or edit by
  /// its own standing would let a token issue a token.
  @@gate("8")
}
